{"url": "https://me.engin.umich.edu/", "final_url": "https://me.engin.umich.edu/", "status": 200, "method": "chromium", "fetched": "2026-11-01", "text": "Skip to content\nADMISSIONS\nABOUT\nACADEMICS\nRESEARCH\nPEOPLE\nNEWS & EVENTS\nQUICKLINKS\nMAKE\nTHE WORLD\nWORK\nBETTER\nNEW MACHINE LEARNING MODEL DRAWS DATA FROM WEARABLE SENSORS TO PREDICT HOW A PHYSICAL THERAPIST WOULD ASSESS BALANCE TRAINING PERFORMANCE.\nBECOME A MECHANICAL ENGINEER\nWhat do you get when you give some of the brightest and most motivated student minds access to world-class faculty and facilities and present them with extremely difficult problems to solve?\nMechanical engineers who have a positive impact on the world around them...\nREAD THE FULL MESSAGE FROM THE CHAIR \u00bb\nNEW ME FACULTY POSITIONS \u00bb\nSPOTLIGHT\nFORGING THE FUTURE\nA storied U-M manufacturing research space has received $4.45M in upgrades, kickstarting a new era centered on cognitive and adaptable manufacturing.\nREAD MORE \u00bb\nREALIZING BREAKTHROUGHS\nModified manufacturing process for EV batteries could enable high ranges and fast charging in cold weather.\nREAD MORE \u00bb\nNOW OPEN: ME BRANDED STORE\nVisit the new U-M Mechanical Engineering webstore to order department branded clothing items, hats, and bags. A portion of the proceeds will be donated to the ME Impact Fund which supports department needs such as critical student support, research, team projects, and more.\nREAD MORE \u00bb\nNEWS\nVIEW ALL NEWS \u00bb\nRESEARCH\nNano-wrinkles and actuation break up infectious biofilms on implants\nOCTOBER 28, 2026\nThe active antibacterial platform improved tissue response in mice and could one day help prevent infections caused by implanted medical devices in humans.\nREAD MORE \u00bb\nCOMMUNITY\nInside the Walter E. Lay Automotive Laboratory\nOCTOBER 27, 2026\nHow the EV Center is Helping Andre Boehman Reshape a Century-Old Proving Ground for the Electric Era\nREAD MORE \u00bb\nRESEARCH\nEmbolism treatment developed at U-M receives FDA clearance\nOCTOBER 26, 2026\nThe H\u0113lo Thrombectomy System, a medical device that quickly and efficiently removes blood clots in the lung for patients with chronic venous thromboembolism, was recently cleared by the U.S. FDA. Commercialization efforts are being led by Endovascular Engineering, a U-M startup.\nREAD MORE \u00bb\nRESEARCH\n$600,000 from ONR to study vortex acoustic waves for long-range ocean communication\nOCTOBER 15, 2026\nA new approach employs acoustic iso-propagation vortices (IPVs), offering a potential solution to communication challenges posed by realistic ocean environments.\nREAD MORE \u00bb\nRESEARCH\nTreating life-threatening blood clots in minutes with swirling sound waves\nOCTOBER 13, 2026\nThe NIH is funding a University of Michigan-led team developing a minimally invasive \u2018vortex ultrasound' device to break up pulmonary embolisms.\nREAD MORE \u00bb\nRESEARCH\nCo-design with the community? Study provides insights on building trust\nSEPTEMBER 29, 2026\nHow and when community stakeholders are engaged shapes participants\u2019 perceived value, learning, and progress according to an assessment of a co-design workshop series.\nREAD MORE \u00bb\nEVENTS\nVIEW ALL EVENTS \u00bb\n3\nNovember\n10:00 AM - 12:00 PM\nWrite with ME!\n5\nNovember\n12:00 PM - 1:00 PM\nAI Engineering MEng Info Session\n5\nNovember\n12:30 PM - 1:30 PM\nDow Information Session: Mechanical Engineering Careers in a Process Industry\nFEATURED VIDEO\nSIMULATING BUS AIRFLOW\nBy simulating how particles exhaled from passengers on a bus would travel through the vehicle under various conditions, researchers at the University of Michigan have been able to develop guidelines for how to operate campus buses more safely during the COVID-19 pandemic.\nMECHANICAL ENGINEERING\nG.G. Brown Laboratory\n2350 Hayward\nAnn Arbor MI 48109\nPhone: (734) 764-2694\nMENU\nAdmissions\nAbout\nAcademics\nResearch\nPeople\nNews & Events\nQuicklinks\nMORE LINKS\nGiving\n